<h2>Why the US matters so much for Indian shrimp exporters</h2>
<p>The scale is worth stating plainly: the United States took 311,948 MT of Indian frozen shrimp in FY2024-25, more than double the next-largest market (China, at 136,164 MT), according to MPEDA's own official press release. Total Indian frozen shrimp exports that year reached 741,529 MT worth roughly USD 5.18 billion, and the US alone accounts for a substantial share of that — within the USA's total seafood import basket from India (USD 2,714.94 million across 346,868 MT), frozen shrimp made up 92.55% of the value. If you're a US importer, you're buying into the anchor market for the entire Indian shrimp industry.</p>

<h2>Getting the form and count right</h2>
<p>Vannamei ships in several forms — HOSO (head-on shell-on), HLSO (headless shell-on), PD (peeled deveined), PUD (peeled undeveined), PDTO (peeled deveined tail-on) and cooked — each with a different yield and price. Count size (pieces per pound) is the other major price driver: 16/20 commands a premium over 71/90 because larger shrimp cost more to farm to size. Specify both before you request a price, and be wary of any comparison between quotes that don't match on form and count.</p>

<h2>The glaze question, explained properly</h2>
<p>This is the single most common source of confusion in shrimp pricing, and it's worth understanding in detail. Glazing is a thin ice coating applied after freezing to prevent freezer burn during storage and transit — typically 0%, 10% or 20% of gross weight. A supplier quoting on gross weight with 20% glaze is effectively offering roughly 20% less actual shrimp than the same number quoted on net weight. We quote net weight and state the glaze percentage separately, and we'd recommend insisting on the same clarity from any supplier you're comparing us against.</p>

<h2>FDA compliance: what to expect</h2>
<p>US food imports run through FSMA (the Food Safety Modernization Act), and two pieces of it apply directly to a shrimp shipment:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>FSVP (Foreign Supplier Verification Program).</strong> Your obligation as the US importer, requiring you to verify your foreign suppliers meet US standards. We provide the documentation and process transparency you'll need to satisfy this.</li>
  <li><strong>Seafood HACCP.</strong> Our processing facility operates under a Hazard Analysis Critical Control Points plan, which FDA can inspect or request records against.</li>
</ul>
<p>Beyond FSMA specifically, FDA maintains import alerts that can place particular products or exporters with a violation history under automatic detention pending testing. We treat pre-shipment antibiotic residue and microbiological testing as standard practice for US-bound shipments rather than an optional add-on — it's cheaper than a detained container every time.</p>

<h2>Planning around a 22-36 day transit</h2>
<p>Transit time depends heavily on which US port you're landing at. East Coast ports — New York/New Jersey and Savannah — typically run 25-35 days from Indian ports. West Coast routing via Panama/Suez or transshipment can be faster, 22-30 days. Houston runs longest at 28-36 days. None of this is a quality issue — frozen product at -18°C holds its 18-24 month shelf life comfortably across any of these windows — but it does mean your reorder planning needs longer lead times than a Gulf or Southeast Asian shipment would require.</p>

