<h2>What determines the price of frozen shrimp</h2>
<p>We never quote a fixed per-kg price in the abstract, because too many variables change it. The main ones:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Count size and form.</strong> Larger counts (16/20, 21/25) and higher-yield forms (PD, PDTO) cost more per pound than smaller counts or HOSO/HLSO.</li>
  <li><strong>Glaze percentage.</strong> A 20%-glaze quote and a 0%-glaze quote on the same nominal weight are not the same amount of shrimp — always confirm the basis.</li>
  <li><strong>Farm-gate conditions.</strong> Feed costs and disease pressure in the growing season (typically peaking with harvest cycles from Andhra Pradesh and neighbouring states) move raw-material cost meaningfully year to year.</li>
  <li><strong>Freight and bunker cost.</strong> Reefer freight to US ports is a substantial share of landed cost, and bunker fuel surcharges move independently of the shrimp market itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Currency.</strong> Quotes are USD-denominated, but our cost base is INR, so USD/INR movement affects where we can price competitively.</li>
</ul>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB or CIF on stated net weight, with glaze percentage disclosed separately. To quote accurately we need: product form, count size, glaze target, packaging (carton configuration or IQF bulk), destination port, and order volume. Antibiotic-residue and microbiological testing requirements (if you want us to pre-test before shipment) also factor into lead time and cost.</p>
