<h2>What actually goes wrong on a US shrimp shipment</h2>
<p>Most disputes and rejections on this route trace back to a handful of recurring issues, and it's worth knowing them before you order rather than after. Antibiotic and microbiological residue detention is the single most consequential one — FDA can place an entire shipment on hold pending re-testing, and if the exporter or specific product line has a prior violation history, that hold becomes close to automatic under an FDA import alert. This is exactly why pre-shipment testing isn't optional practice for us on US-bound lots: it's materially cheaper than a detained reefer container sitting at port accruing demurrage while a lab result comes back.</p>
<p>Count-size disputes are the second most common friction point. Count tolerance bands (the acceptable spread of pieces-per-pound around a stated count, e.g. 16/20) are standard in the trade, but buyers who haven't seen a shipment before sometimes expect zero variance, which isn't how farmed shrimp grading works — grading is by sample, not every individual piece. We state the tolerance band up front so there's no surprise at receiving inspection.</p>
<p>Glaze-weight disputes are the third recurring issue, and almost always trace back to a quote that didn't clearly state net vs. gross weight at the outset — see the glaze section above. On the cold-chain side, reefer temperature excursions during the 22-36 day transit (a container losing power at a transshipment port, for example) are rare but do happen; we can supply a reefer temperature data logger on request so you have an objective record if a claim ever needs to be made.</p>
<p>The single biggest mistake we see from first-time US buyers is treating "shrimp" as a single product with one price. Comparing a HOSO 71/90 quote against a PD 16/20 quote and concluding one supplier is cheaper is comparing two different products. The second most common mistake is underestimating how much lead time FSVP paperwork needs on a first order — start that conversation with your compliance team before you place the order, not after.</p>
